Sylvester's benchmark resume, in his Drive as "Kevin Resume.pdf" (Drive id `16PJ08bdx5x7gjOOcGlohkZdza1tml1T7`, re-fetch via [[reference-gdrive-mcp]]; cached ~/.gdrive-mcp-cache). Kevin K. Manu — Rutgers (BS Economics 2018, business admin minor 2023-24), Bloomberg equity data analyst → Morgan Stanley DevOps L3 → Notables product/strategy → JPMorgan Chase Software Engineer II (May 2023).

**Now at MICROSOFT** (LinkedIn linkedin.com/in/kevinmanu, NYC metro, Microsoft-issued GitHub Copilot cert Jun 2026). The Drive PDF is the JPMC-era version, i.e. **this is the resume that moved him JPMC → Microsoft — a proven artifact, not a theoretical one.** Sylvester was right about Microsoft; the PDF alone was stale.

**WARM PATH, worth more than the formatting:** Sylvester has Kevin's resume, so he knows him. Both NJ, both Rutgers (Kevin BS + minor, Sylvester full-stack cert 2024), Kevin is a 2000-follower NYC-metro Microsoft engineer. A referral from him beats any cold application — referrals convert several times better. ACT ON THIS before optimizing PDFs further. Also: Kevin's own trajectory (Bloomberg analyst → DevOps → product → SWE) is proof the non-CS-degree pivot works, which is exactly Sylvester's path.

**Why it works — the formula to copy (structure, never content):**
- 1 page, plain text, standard headers: CAREER SUMMARY / EXPERIENCE / EDUCATION & CERTIFICATIONS / SOFTWARE & SKILLS. No tables, no columns, no graphics.
- Opens with a **positioning line**, not an objective: "Product-oriented technologist with 6+ years spanning software engineering, product management, and strategy…" — claims a *category* and a span.
- **Every single bullet carries a number**: 30% latency, 47% retrieval, $150K tech debt saved, $2M raised, $1.2M partnership closed, 73% fewer incidents, 38% help-desk reduction, 70% fewer client errors, 20% onboarding lift, 5 data centers. Zero duty-description bullets.
- Bullet shape: **strong verb + specific technology + measurable business outcome** ("Executed a migration of legacy ETL workflows from Informatica to Pentaho, saving $150,000 in technical debt").
- Named tools inside the bullets (Azure Load Testing, Grafana, REST APIs, S3, Oracle) so ATS keyword matching happens in context, not just in a skills list.
- Skills section is **categorized**: Languages / Cloud & Data / DevOps & Monitoring / Frameworks / Tools.

**Sylvester's gap vs this (he has the raw material, it's presentation):** his real metrics — 40% faster scan rendering, 15+ devices at 18% faster rollout, $14K vendor savings, $300K production orders, 10% margin lift, $19K closed revenue, 200+ staff supported — are equal-caliber. What's missing is (a) a positioning summary line, (b) a metric on EVERY bullet, (c) named tools inside bullets rather than only in a skills list, (d) categorized skills. Healthcare-infra positioning is his differentiator (HMH + SailPoint/Okta + pharma), and it doubles as CASPA healthcare experience — see [[project-pa-school]], [[project-job-hunt-tailor]].

Never copy Kevin's bullets or claims — same *shape*, Sylvester's own true facts only.
